00:28:41.960 --> 00:28:43.730
is this is just very sad.

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:44.743
There's no like,

00:28:45.580 --> 00:28:48.480
it's basically just like
I'm praying for you, guys,

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:49.550
this is very sad.

00:28:49.550 --> 00:28:52.653
I hope you find the faith,
those kinds of responses.

00:28:53.680 --> 00:28:56.310
He was a very, very reminiscent of the way

00:28:56.310 --> 00:28:59.120
that we interacted with him

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:03.100
and it just reminds me how
much where we were at in life

00:29:03.100 --> 00:29:07.490
and what we were doing was
informing our responses

00:29:07.490 --> 00:29:10.760
because we were literally raising support

00:29:11.690 --> 00:29:13.493
to go on staff full time.

00:29:15.344 --> 00:29:18.210
And I had probably, you were
in the very middle of it,

00:29:18.210 --> 00:29:19.520
I was coming to the end.

00:29:19.520 --> 00:29:24.120
This giant sort of left turn in your life

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:27.450
where you've thrown, you've
left your previous job

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:30.120
and you're putting it
all on the line for Jesus

00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:33.030
and then you go and you talk to a friend

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:35.583
who you really respect and we probably,

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:40.760
we had the deepest sort of
intellectual conversations

00:29:40.760 --> 00:29:43.343
about our faith even in
high school with this guy.

00:29:44.540 --> 00:29:47.750
We talked about things
on as deep of a level

00:29:47.750 --> 00:29:49.970
as we could at the time

00:29:49.970 --> 00:29:53.300
and in high school, I remember
those conversations starting.

00:29:53.300 --> 00:29:54.343
So, we just always kinda had this

00:29:54.343 --> 00:29:56.493
sort of philosophical
thing going with him.

00:29:57.530 --> 00:30:00.520
And then for him to have
these really deep questions

00:30:00.520 --> 00:30:03.020
that were kinda tearing
at the roots of our faith,

00:30:04.350 --> 00:30:06.510
I remember me and you talking to him

00:30:06.510 --> 00:30:09.900
and then talking to each
other and it was just like,

00:30:09.900 --> 00:30:12.120
I think the way you
described it in a text to him

00:30:12.120 --> 00:30:14.640
the other day was like, we just had,

00:30:14.640 --> 00:30:18.844
there was zero openness to
what you were going through.

00:30:18.844 --> 00:30:22.830
There was a complete inability

00:30:22.830 --> 00:30:25.340
to turn the questions that
you were asking yourself

00:30:25.340 --> 00:30:28.610
onto ourselves because there
was way too much at stake

00:30:28.610 --> 00:30:29.443
at the time.

00:30:29.443 --> 00:30:30.276
Yeah.

00:30:30.276 --> 00:30:31.540
What are we, gonna
suddenly stop races at port

00:30:31.540 --> 00:30:33.370
and tell everybody that who
just gave us all this money

00:30:33.370 --> 00:30:35.286
that oh, by the way, guys,

00:30:35.286 --> 00:30:37.593
I'm really questioning the
foundations of this whole thing.

00:30:37.593 --> 00:30:39.050
It's like I'm not gonna go there.

00:30:39.050 --> 00:30:42.170
So, I'm not even gonna allow
that thought to enter my mind.

00:30:42.170 --> 00:30:44.130
Yeah, so, in my recollection

00:30:44.130 --> 00:30:48.460
was we were as polite as we could to be

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:53.460
in trying to help him turn a corner,

00:30:54.240 --> 00:30:55.940
to double back

00:30:56.930 --> 00:30:58.140
and that was it.

00:30:58.140 --> 00:31:01.920
'Cause that's what we were supposed to do.

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:06.313
So, it wasn't an actual conversation.

00:31:07.360 --> 00:31:11.570
And he felt that and he knew that.

00:31:11.570 --> 00:31:14.420
He was also gracious because
in his journal entry,

00:31:14.420 --> 00:31:17.347
he basically said, "I completely get

00:31:17.347 --> 00:31:18.587
"where these guys are coming from

00:31:18.587 --> 00:31:20.567
"because it's where I came from."

00:31:21.460 --> 00:31:22.800
And that's why, and so--

00:31:22.800 --> 00:31:24.750
Yeah, I think he said that he did

00:31:24.750 --> 00:31:26.830
to others what we were doing to him.

00:31:26.830 --> 00:31:28.310
And so all that to say that

00:31:31.190 --> 00:31:35.500
any sort of dismissive comments

00:31:35.500 --> 00:31:37.100
that we're getting about our story,

00:31:37.100 --> 00:31:39.000
which again, is a very small minority,

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:43.130
it's more than justified.

00:31:43.130 --> 00:31:47.150
We deserve every bit of
it because we've done it,

00:31:47.150 --> 00:31:51.010
we've been there and that,

00:31:51.010 --> 00:31:52.970
and I remember thinking about him

00:31:52.970 --> 00:31:57.677
in thinking about I was like,
"Where did you go wrong?"

00:31:59.460 --> 00:32:03.713
Like, I think he hung out
with the wrong people.

00:32:04.750 --> 00:32:07.100
I was trying to figure out,

00:32:07.100 --> 00:32:09.590
I was trying to get to
the bottom of why he,

00:32:09.590 --> 00:32:14.590
someone who had a very
real strong informed faith

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:19.020
was suddenly questioning it

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:21.387
and I was just like,
"Where did he screw up?

00:32:21.387 --> 00:32:23.387
"Let me find that.

00:32:23.387 --> 00:32:25.300
"Let me find where he screwed up."

00:32:25.300 --> 00:32:26.743
I never could figure it out.

00:32:29.210 --> 00:32:31.860
So, anyway, I just wanted
to acknowledge that,

00:32:31.860 --> 00:32:34.560
that we've done that and we've been there.

00:32:34.560 --> 00:32:37.130
Yeah, so, I think there are people

00:32:37.130 --> 00:32:40.267
who are coming to our defense
when they see people say,

00:32:40.267 --> 00:32:41.920
"It makes me sad."

00:32:41.920 --> 00:32:45.210
And I saw someone make the point

00:32:45.210 --> 00:32:47.150
I don't think people realize when they say

00:32:47.150 --> 00:32:48.690
that it makes them sad

00:32:48.690 --> 00:32:52.260
when someone else shares
their personal story

00:32:52.260 --> 00:32:54.530
that that's manipulative.

00:32:54.530 --> 00:32:57.600
But I get it.

00:32:57.600 --> 00:33:02.330
I understand why it makes,

00:33:02.330 --> 00:33:05.540
if you have a certain worldview,

00:33:05.540 --> 00:33:07.846
and it made me sad before
for other people, so--

00:33:07.846 --> 00:33:09.346
Yeah, 100%.

00:33:11.747 --> 00:33:13.350
But I do think it's helpful.

00:33:13.350 --> 00:33:15.880
I think it's helpful and
listen, I'm not saying this

00:33:15.880 --> 00:33:17.583
but I think this could be,

00:33:19.200 --> 00:33:22.600
this could help those of you
who have said this is sad

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:25.030
to understand why it's not,

00:33:25.030 --> 00:33:27.450
it's difficult to not
take that as an insult

00:33:28.340 --> 00:33:31.200
because if I were to say it's sad

00:33:31.200 --> 00:33:32.650
that you that you've based your life

00:33:32.650 --> 00:33:34.483
on something that's not true.

00:33:34.483 --> 00:33:37.510
That's offensive, that's an insult, right?

00:33:37.510 --> 00:33:39.260
To say that to you.

00:33:39.260 --> 00:33:43.370
I'm not saying that but
if I were to say that,

00:33:43.370 --> 00:33:44.300
that would be offensive,

00:33:44.300 --> 00:33:47.100
it would be insulting,
it would be dismissive.

00:33:47.100 --> 00:33:49.070
And so, I think the same thing is it's sad

00:33:49.070 --> 00:33:50.700
that you guys are moving
away from this thing

00:33:50.700 --> 00:33:51.940
that I know to be true

00:33:53.740 --> 00:33:57.610
and it's sad that your eternal
destiny is in question.

00:33:57.610 --> 00:33:59.660
I understand where you're coming from

00:33:59.660 --> 00:34:01.950
and according to your worldview,

00:34:01.950 --> 00:34:05.980
this is pretty much the only
response that you could have

00:34:05.980 --> 00:34:08.430
but to communicate it in that way

00:34:08.430 --> 00:34:11.100
and not understand why that's
insulting and dismissive

00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:13.193
and people are gonna
perceive it in that way,

00:34:14.290 --> 00:34:17.130
hopefully, I've shown a
little bit of light on that.

00:34:17.130 --> 00:34:19.720
I think another thing
that we didn't anticipate

00:34:19.720 --> 00:34:24.720
was that people, not that
people were gonna come out

00:34:25.090 --> 00:34:29.070
of the woodwork in order to text or email

00:34:29.070 --> 00:34:31.370
or try to Facebook messages

00:34:31.370 --> 00:34:33.740
even though we're not on Facebook anymore,

00:34:33.740 --> 00:34:38.740
about things is, well, it's
not that that surprises

00:34:39.520 --> 00:34:44.333
but they would then reach out to our wives

00:34:45.400 --> 00:34:47.800
or our extended families.

00:34:47.800 --> 00:34:49.823
And so,

00:34:51.500 --> 00:34:53.300
that was something that,

00:34:53.300 --> 00:34:55.310
I don't know if it would have changed how

00:34:55.310 --> 00:34:56.360
or if we would have done it

00:34:56.360 --> 00:34:59.993
but it's not something that
we specifically anticipated.

00:35:02.680 --> 00:35:05.450
It's like, I mean Christy
would get a bunch of messages

00:35:05.450 --> 00:35:07.070
from people she hadn't
heard from in a while

00:35:07.070 --> 00:35:08.370
and I know they come from a place

00:35:08.370 --> 00:35:11.145
of really caring but it's like,

00:35:11.145 --> 00:35:14.810
now, she's got a field
questions from people

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:18.980
that are like, it was
basically like, are you okay?

00:35:18.980 --> 00:35:21.030
Are you guys, okay?

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:23.320
And I was like, Christy, you know,

00:35:23.320 --> 00:35:25.143
the thing that I'm realizing is that,

00:35:27.550 --> 00:35:30.590
when I tell the story
of us in the minivan,

00:35:30.590 --> 00:35:33.360
like in tears, not going in a church

00:35:33.360 --> 00:35:38.240
and trying to figure where we
were and where we each were

00:35:38.240 --> 00:35:39.440
and all those type of things

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:43.040
and the anguish from that
particular story I told,

00:35:43.040 --> 00:35:44.593
well, that was two years ago.

00:35:45.610 --> 00:35:50.610
It's like, we're in a
different place than that.

00:35:50.680 --> 00:35:51.650
We're not sitting,

00:35:51.650 --> 00:35:53.720
we don't have the minivan anymore

00:35:53.720 --> 00:35:56.720
and even if we did,
we're not sitting in it

00:35:56.720 --> 00:35:58.520
weeping every Sunday.

00:35:58.520 --> 00:36:02.023
We're in a much healthier,

00:36:03.810 --> 00:36:07.790
healthy is not the right word
but like we're in a good place

00:36:07.790 --> 00:36:11.700
and people, when you share this story

00:36:11.700 --> 00:36:14.010
and if the last time they talked to you

00:36:14.010 --> 00:36:16.430
was three, four, five,
six, seven years ago,

00:36:16.430 --> 00:36:19.040
it's like, well, they don't
they don't know when that was

00:36:19.040 --> 00:36:21.330
so it's like, did this just happen?

00:36:21.330 --> 00:36:22.620
You know?

00:36:22.620 --> 00:36:25.070
And really, the first
thing Christy learned

00:36:25.070 --> 00:36:27.350
was the first question
she would ask a response

00:36:27.350 --> 00:36:29.490
to the people that she would engage with

00:36:29.490 --> 00:36:33.980
was have you actually
listened to the podcast?

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:36.720
'Cause a lot of people,
they would hear from someone

00:36:36.720 --> 00:36:38.320
and they would have concern

00:36:38.320 --> 00:36:41.210
but they wouldn't have
listened to it themselves.

00:36:41.210 --> 00:36:43.320
And so that's an important point.

00:36:43.320 --> 00:36:45.350
It's like I'm not gonna engage

00:36:45.350 --> 00:36:47.450
if you don't really know
all the details already.

00:36:47.450 --> 00:36:51.850
I can't sit here and retell
you all these on Facebook.

00:36:51.850 --> 00:36:54.120
So, even from a purely
practical standpoint,

00:36:54.120 --> 00:36:55.596
it's kinda like,

00:36:55.596 --> 00:36:58.520
there's a handful or
maybe more than handful

00:36:58.520 --> 00:36:59.820
of like, oh we got a...

00:37:00.710 --> 00:37:03.200
I find myself in a
conversation with someone

00:37:03.200 --> 00:37:07.640
and it's like they're bringing
some assumptions to the table

00:37:07.640 --> 00:37:10.500
that like we're in trouble.

00:37:10.500 --> 00:37:11.333
Yeah.

00:37:11.333 --> 00:37:13.095
And that's not the case.

00:37:13.095 --> 00:37:14.793
Yeah, I didn't anticipate,

00:37:16.320 --> 00:37:18.130
I mean, I should have thought about it

00:37:18.130 --> 00:37:19.890
but I was thinking about a lot of things

00:37:19.890 --> 00:37:24.890
leading up to us talking
and I did not anticipate,

00:37:28.370 --> 00:37:30.400
given the nature of a small town

00:37:31.580 --> 00:37:33.883
and a community of people who,

00:37:34.890 --> 00:37:37.780
at least in the circles that
my family runs in back home,

00:37:37.780 --> 00:37:42.553
it's like, we're talking about
a Christian circle of people,

00:37:42.553 --> 00:37:44.653
a very well-connected group of people.

00:37:46.790 --> 00:37:48.060
Both my family and Jessie's family.

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:50.590
So it's like the amount of people

00:37:50.590 --> 00:37:55.010
who have kinda come up
to them saying things,

00:37:55.010 --> 00:37:56.850
making assumptions, again,

00:37:56.850 --> 00:38:00.143
having not listened to
the podcast in many cases.

00:38:01.630 --> 00:38:03.470
Yeah, I feel like I could
have prepared them better.

00:38:03.470 --> 00:38:04.520
I feel bad about that.

00:38:04.520 --> 00:38:08.230
I think that's been a source
of some anxiety for me as well

00:38:09.410 --> 00:38:10.310
as I've thought about this

00:38:10.310 --> 00:38:12.780
because they knew that we
were gonna talk about it

00:38:12.780 --> 00:38:13.873
to some degree.

00:38:15.510 --> 00:38:16.343
Some more than others.

00:38:16.343 --> 00:38:18.120
They're back there on
kind of the frontlines.

00:38:18.120 --> 00:38:19.000
It's like we've been dealing

00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:20.810
with a lot of the online stuff.

00:38:20.810 --> 00:38:21.643
Nobody comes up

00:38:21.643 --> 00:38:22.993
to us on the street.
But nobody in LA.

00:38:23.910 --> 00:38:24.810
Yeah.

00:38:24.810 --> 00:38:25.903
But they'll see somebody
at the grocery store.

00:38:25.903 --> 00:38:27.170
You're not sitting on a
restaurant and a grocery store

00:38:27.170 --> 00:38:29.763
and somebody's gonna come up to, you know.

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:38.040
Yeah, so, I guess we could
have done better on that front.

00:38:39.430 --> 00:38:43.000
But I wanted to talk
about what you were saying

00:38:44.800 --> 00:38:45.860
with the number of,

00:38:45.860 --> 00:38:47.281
the way that I interacted with the number

00:38:47.281 --> 00:38:49.400
of like stories that people were telling

00:38:49.400 --> 00:38:51.523
and a number of people who related.

00:38:52.510 --> 00:38:56.170
Now, my initial reaction
was one, this is awesome,

00:38:56.170 --> 00:38:57.763
this is super encouraging.

00:39:00.580 --> 00:39:02.750
Like you said, there's a few times

00:39:02.750 --> 00:39:03.860
where I was just reading people's stories,

00:39:03.860 --> 00:39:06.410
it was very emotionally,

00:39:06.410 --> 00:39:07.610
it's a very emotional thing.

00:39:07.610 --> 00:39:11.530
It's like people have been
through some shit, man.

00:39:11.530 --> 00:39:12.890
Yeah.

00:39:12.890 --> 00:39:15.449
In contrast to our stories.

00:39:15.449 --> 00:39:17.630
Like I said, it was very difficult

00:39:17.630 --> 00:39:20.150
to kinda move out of that world

00:39:20.150 --> 00:39:25.150
but that world we benefited
personally from that world

00:39:25.690 --> 00:39:29.440
in a lot of ways that other
people have a lot more trauma

00:39:29.440 --> 00:39:30.940
depending on their background.

00:39:31.990 --> 00:39:34.930
And some of those stories
are just heart-wrenching.

00:39:34.930 --> 00:39:38.450
But the way that again, so there's,

00:39:38.450 --> 00:39:40.210
Just sort of my heart reaction to it

00:39:40.210 --> 00:39:43.370
which is this is great and
then there's my head reaction

00:39:43.370 --> 00:39:45.920
to it which is like what
are we gonna do with this?

00:39:47.270 --> 00:39:49.430
I'm always thinking about
what the next thing is

00:39:49.430 --> 00:39:51.447
that we're gonna do and--

00:39:52.980 --> 00:39:54.335
Yeah, I thought about that too.

00:39:54.335 --> 00:39:59.335
When you got all these
people sharing this story

00:40:00.120 --> 00:40:01.740
and there feels like there's this momentum

00:40:01.740 --> 00:40:04.443
and there feels like there is this,

00:40:07.445 --> 00:40:10.120
we've all kind of left something

00:40:11.160 --> 00:40:14.350
that was very organized and
was a very particular movement

00:40:14.350 --> 00:40:18.300
into this thing that it's
just pretty nebulous,

00:40:18.300 --> 00:40:22.030
what is this life after Christian faith.

00:40:22.030 --> 00:40:23.950
Yeah, are you talking about thoughts

00:40:23.950 --> 00:40:25.370
that enter your mind like,

00:40:25.370 --> 00:40:27.938
well, should we organize something?

00:40:27.938 --> 00:40:29.458
No, no, no, no, no, no.

00:40:29.458 --> 00:40:31.087
I wanna be clear, I wanna be clear.

00:40:31.087 --> 00:40:36.087
I'm too old and just wise enough

00:40:36.740 --> 00:40:38.640
to know that that would
be a horrible idea.

00:40:38.640 --> 00:40:40.840
I'm not talking about a Rhett Link church.

00:40:40.840 --> 00:40:43.510
I'm talking about like a specific, like,

00:40:43.510 --> 00:40:45.610
oh, let's complexion of this podcast

00:40:45.610 --> 00:40:48.250
to have some programmatic

00:40:49.830 --> 00:40:51.591
addressing of some of these things.

00:40:51.591 --> 00:40:53.250
I would say in a more general sense,

00:40:53.250 --> 00:40:54.890
it's just asking the question

00:40:54.890 --> 00:40:59.890
of is there a project
associated with this, right?

00:41:00.220 --> 00:41:01.053
Mm-hm.

00:41:01.053 --> 00:41:03.550
That's one question that enters my mind,

00:41:03.550 --> 00:41:05.193
is like is there a project?

00:41:06.090 --> 00:41:08.620
I don't know what that is
but you got all these people,

00:41:08.620 --> 00:41:12.530
they've got their stories,
there's this momentum,

00:41:12.530 --> 00:41:16.420
we've tapped into something
and so I feel this pressure

00:41:16.420 --> 00:41:17.360
to sort of figure that out.

00:41:17.360 --> 00:41:22.360
At the same time, I'm
feeling this pressure

00:41:23.110 --> 00:41:25.710
to like you said, this has sort
of been a kick in the pants

00:41:25.710 --> 00:41:30.710
to like sort of re-engage spiritually.

00:41:34.010 --> 00:41:37.101
For me, that's been true but it's also,

00:41:37.101 --> 00:41:39.351
kinda the way that it's
registered with me is

00:41:41.430 --> 00:41:45.400
I threw out this deconstruction story

00:41:46.240 --> 00:41:50.313
and talked about what I
don't believe anymore.

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:53.710
I talked a little bit
about what I do believe now

00:41:53.710 --> 00:41:55.650
but what I do believe now is held

00:41:55.650 --> 00:42:00.063
with a much lower level
of conviction and passion.

00:42:02.310 --> 00:42:05.200
Because it's more of a position
of openness and curiosity

00:42:05.200 --> 00:42:08.560
but I don't wanna be in
that position perpetually

00:42:08.560 --> 00:42:10.393
and never embrace anything, right?

00:42:11.340 --> 00:42:15.063
But there was this sort of
like, people like, you just,

00:42:16.600 --> 00:42:18.790
again, you just threw this
rock into the hornet's nest

00:42:18.790 --> 00:42:22.320
and now, you got these
hornets flying around

00:42:22.320 --> 00:42:23.720
and some of them are coming up to you

00:42:23.720 --> 00:42:25.173
and asking, well, what now?

00:42:26.500 --> 00:42:27.333
You know?

00:42:27.333 --> 00:42:28.166
Mm-hm.

00:42:28.166 --> 00:42:29.450
And I'm not trying to put on the mantle

00:42:29.450 --> 00:42:30.790
of some spiritual leader.

00:42:30.790 --> 00:42:33.080
That's the last thing that I
want to do because that's not,

00:42:33.080 --> 00:42:36.770
it's very much the last
thing that I ever want to do.

00:42:36.770 --> 00:42:39.150
But there is this part
of me that's just like,

00:42:39.150 --> 00:42:41.803
well, you should find some answers.

00:42:45.346 --> 00:42:46.179
You should find some answers

00:42:46.179 --> 00:42:48.760
and then you should relay those answers.

00:42:48.760 --> 00:42:52.460
And again, I just feel like
that is rooted in my ego.

00:42:52.460 --> 00:42:55.000
I don't think that that's
rooted in anything good.

00:42:55.000 --> 00:42:56.840
I don't think that there's,

00:42:56.840 --> 00:42:59.863
I think it's very much about a personal,

00:43:00.990 --> 00:43:04.340
personal interest and
personal self-justification.

00:43:04.340 --> 00:43:07.300
I don't think that there's any,

00:43:07.300 --> 00:43:08.800
I should be leaning into that.

00:43:11.230 --> 00:43:14.920
That doesn't mean that I
shouldn't be personally seeking

00:43:14.920 --> 00:43:19.920
and being open and being open
to whatever is next for me,

00:43:23.120 --> 00:43:24.380
which I think that I am

00:43:24.380 --> 00:43:29.100
but doing that because I
want to then present myself

00:43:29.100 --> 00:43:30.772
to a group of people
who are listening is a,

00:43:30.772 --> 00:43:32.090
and the interesting thing is--

00:43:32.090 --> 00:43:32.923
It's a trap.

00:43:32.923 --> 00:43:37.880
Ironically, that's what
ministry is for so many people.

00:43:38.950 --> 00:43:40.880
That's what any position of leadership is

00:43:40.880 --> 00:43:44.410
but I've got a lot of
friends who are pastors

00:43:44.410 --> 00:43:46.030
and I've had a lot of
friends who are pastors

00:43:46.030 --> 00:43:51.030
and some of them in
their most honest moments

00:43:51.280 --> 00:43:52.900
have relayed to me

00:43:52.900 --> 00:43:57.170
that there is this sort of
self-fulfilling prophecy.

00:43:57.170 --> 00:43:59.201
I don't know, that's probably
not the right terminology

00:43:59.201 --> 00:44:02.090
but there is this idea that now,

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:05.330
I am the one that you
are coming to for answers

00:44:05.330 --> 00:44:08.010
and so I must create the answers.

00:44:08.010 --> 00:44:09.650
I must have the answers.

00:44:09.650 --> 00:44:11.990
I must live the life.

00:44:11.990 --> 00:44:15.370
I must have the appearance
of having it figured out

00:44:15.370 --> 00:44:17.770
because that is what you expect of me

00:44:17.770 --> 00:44:19.400
and I think that a lot of pastors

00:44:19.400 --> 00:44:21.370
are just kinda stuck in this--

00:44:21.370 --> 00:44:22.210
It's tough.

00:44:22.210 --> 00:44:25.137
Hamster wheel of appearances and kinda,

00:44:25.137 --> 00:44:26.550
and the church, in a lot of ways,

00:44:26.550 --> 00:44:30.720
has set it up that you can't screw up.

00:44:30.720 --> 00:44:32.960
You can't be a human.

00:44:32.960 --> 00:44:34.740
You can't have imperfections

00:44:34.740 --> 00:44:36.760
especially if you're in leadership,

00:44:36.760 --> 00:44:38.690
especially if you're the pastor.

00:44:38.690 --> 00:44:39.523
Right.

00:44:40.410 --> 00:44:43.202
Yeah, I feel for them.

00:44:43.202 --> 00:44:48.202
But I'll draw an
analogous scenario for us.

00:44:52.270 --> 00:44:56.070
I was on a Valentine's date
with Christy and first of all,

00:44:56.070 --> 00:44:57.870
there's a story that
I'm not gonna tell now.

00:44:57.870 --> 00:44:59.830
Maybe I'll tell it next
week when it's like,

00:44:59.830 --> 00:45:02.270
when we have more space for
this so don't let me forget.

00:45:02.270 --> 00:45:04.630
I got this funny thing that happened.

00:45:04.630 --> 00:45:06.480
But that's kind of my point.

00:45:06.480 --> 00:45:09.650
I was in a dinner and
another weird thing happened

00:45:09.650 --> 00:45:10.950
that I'm not referring to,

00:45:12.530 --> 00:45:15.950
and I made a joke to Christy at dinner

00:45:15.950 --> 00:45:18.450
and I was like, "Oh, you know I'm going

00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:20.120
to talk about this on 'Ear Biscuits'."

00:45:20.120 --> 00:45:21.393
And it was like,

00:45:22.390 --> 00:45:27.210
she was like, "Uh-uh, are you serious?"

00:45:27.210 --> 00:45:29.780
It's like, and it wasn't
that she didn't want me

00:45:29.780 --> 00:45:30.760
to share a funny story.

00:45:30.760 --> 00:45:32.030
It's just that we're on a date.

00:45:32.030 --> 00:45:34.760
This is about me and her
and if I'm sitting there,

00:45:34.760 --> 00:45:35.870
the moment something happens,

00:45:35.870 --> 00:45:38.850
I'm like, "Oh, yeah, I got
a 'Ear Biscuit' story."

00:45:38.850 --> 00:45:43.630
Then that robs the intimacy of the moment.

00:45:43.630 --> 00:45:47.401
It's like, well, we just had
a funny moment, you know?

00:45:47.401 --> 00:45:49.350
We experienced a funny moment

00:45:49.350 --> 00:45:51.570
that you can then have
on an audience later.

00:45:51.570 --> 00:45:56.010
And I was like, actually,
I was just joking

00:45:56.010 --> 00:45:58.100
because I that was my joke, Christy,

00:45:58.100 --> 00:45:59.653
well, that was my defense.

00:46:00.850 --> 00:46:01.683
It's just a joke

00:46:01.683 --> 00:46:03.953
but I'm talking about it on
"Ear Biscuits" right now.

00:46:05.860 --> 00:46:07.610
No, I'm talking about--

00:46:07.610 --> 00:46:08.443
Talking about.

00:46:08.443 --> 00:46:09.630
Talking about it but I'm
not talking about it.

00:46:09.630 --> 00:46:10.470
Okay.

00:46:10.470 --> 00:46:12.630
But maybe next week,
I'll share another story

00:46:12.630 --> 00:46:13.863
but not this one because
it's off limits now.

00:46:13.863 --> 00:46:16.600
Because that's a sacred, precious.

00:46:16.600 --> 00:46:18.280
Just between you and Christy.

00:46:18.280 --> 00:46:20.040
But my point,

00:46:20.040 --> 00:46:22.080
I hear your point when it comes to like,

00:46:22.080 --> 00:46:23.720
am I gonna pursue?

00:46:23.720 --> 00:46:27.320
Am I gonna like, let's just
say it's find an answer

00:46:27.320 --> 00:46:32.320
to a specific spiritual
philosophical question for yourself.

00:46:32.810 --> 00:46:34.097
Are you gonna find that question

00:46:34.097 --> 00:46:36.850
just so you can turn around

00:46:36.850 --> 00:46:41.370
and prove that you have an
answer on "Ear Biscuits"?

00:46:41.370 --> 00:46:43.490
Like that's dangerous, that is a trap.

00:46:43.490 --> 00:46:44.323
You know, it's like--

00:46:44.323 --> 00:46:45.670
It's a trap for certain personalities.

00:46:45.670 --> 00:46:49.690
My personality, listen, it's a trap for me

00:46:49.690 --> 00:46:52.260
and it's one of the ways

00:46:52.260 --> 00:46:55.690
that I interacted with responsibilities

00:46:55.690 --> 00:46:56.523
in the church, right?

00:46:56.523 --> 00:46:57.750
It's like, yeah, of course,

00:46:57.750 --> 00:47:00.010
I'm gonna lead a Bible study, right?

00:47:00.010 --> 00:47:01.210
And, of course, every once in a while,

00:47:01.210 --> 00:47:03.540
if they asked me, I'll preach.

00:47:03.540 --> 00:47:05.277
Give a talk on something.

00:47:07.730 --> 00:47:09.380
And I think there's a,

00:47:09.380 --> 00:47:10.543
ironically,

00:47:13.270 --> 00:47:14.490
the way and, of course,

00:47:14.490 --> 00:47:16.210
our experiences with
the evangelical church

00:47:16.210 --> 00:47:17.500
but the way the evangelical church

00:47:17.500 --> 00:47:22.500
is structured at this
point is typically men

00:47:22.570 --> 00:47:24.410
who are Type A personalities,

00:47:24.410 --> 00:47:29.190
who would otherwise make great
CEOs make really good pastors

00:47:30.250 --> 00:47:32.200
in the system that is currently set up.

00:47:33.360 --> 00:47:35.100
And they're charismatic leaders,

00:47:35.100 --> 00:47:36.840
not charismatic in the theological sense

00:47:36.840 --> 00:47:39.400
but just winning personalities.

00:47:39.400 --> 00:47:40.860
They're smart, they're insightful,

00:47:40.860 --> 00:47:43.613
they know how to relate, they
know how to tell a good story.

00:47:44.620 --> 00:47:47.980
And they quickly find hundreds,

00:47:47.980 --> 00:47:51.370
if not thousands of people who want in

00:47:52.420 --> 00:47:57.420
and I think it just
creates a really unhealthy,

00:47:57.940 --> 00:48:02.853
I know for me, personally, if
we had a stayed in ministry,

00:48:03.690 --> 00:48:07.130
I'm sure that we would have
taken some sort of path

00:48:08.010 --> 00:48:12.560
in leadership and I think I operated

00:48:12.560 --> 00:48:15.810
in a really unhealthy place
for a really long time.

00:48:15.810 --> 00:48:17.793
I know I did, I described it.

00:48:17.793 --> 00:48:20.350
A as a worship leader.

00:48:20.350 --> 00:48:22.250
Yeah, but the interesting thing

00:48:22.250 --> 00:48:26.470
is that to get back to
the what we're gonna do

00:48:26.470 --> 00:48:29.273
with this momentum or whatever it is,

00:48:30.770 --> 00:48:32.270
and this is kinda what I was talking about

00:48:32.270 --> 00:48:35.170
in therapy last night is finding a way

00:48:35.170 --> 00:48:40.170
to just lean in to the
personal nature of the process.

00:48:44.270 --> 00:48:46.563
But it not being about a movement.

00:48:47.690 --> 00:48:49.130
It not being about

00:48:51.270 --> 00:48:54.750
being right, being justified,

00:48:54.750 --> 00:48:58.320
being the Bible answer man.

00:48:58.320 --> 00:48:59.570
You know what I'm saying?

00:48:59.570 --> 00:49:03.130
And having an answer and
having a reason for everything

00:49:03.130 --> 00:49:05.490
because very quickly, almost immediately,

00:49:05.490 --> 00:49:06.840
that just becomes about me.

00:49:07.710 --> 00:49:12.500
It just becomes about me
wanting to justify myself

00:49:12.500 --> 00:49:14.513
and to get your approval.

00:49:16.830 --> 00:49:19.233
The whole process that I've
been going through in therapy

00:49:19.233 --> 00:49:22.560
is leaning away from that
and being like it's not,

00:49:22.560 --> 00:49:24.826
no, no, no, it's not
about people's approval.

00:49:24.826 --> 00:49:27.483
It's not about what
people think about you.

00:49:28.770 --> 00:49:32.640
It's about figuring out
what is actually happening

00:49:33.490 --> 00:49:34.363
in your heart.

00:49:37.120 --> 00:49:40.740
So, so my desire for when
we talk about these things

00:49:40.740 --> 00:49:42.920
on the podcast is just it to,

00:49:42.920 --> 00:49:46.120
I'm not saying we shouldn't
answer a question or two,

00:49:46.120 --> 00:49:48.320
at some point, even now, I don't know,

00:49:48.320 --> 00:49:50.620
probably not now, we're
almost out of time but

00:49:52.900 --> 00:49:55.600
I want the tone of that and
the purpose of that to be,

00:49:56.470 --> 00:49:58.863
hey, I'm not an expert,

00:50:00.400 --> 00:50:03.690
just a couple of guys
trying to figure out life,

00:50:03.690 --> 00:50:06.723
trying to move closer to
the truth, doing our best.

00:50:08.340 --> 00:50:10.543
Yeah, for me, like I said,

00:50:10.543 --> 00:50:15.203
the process of share my
story really helped me,

00:50:16.130 --> 00:50:19.060
almost internalize some conclusions

00:50:19.060 --> 00:50:23.290
about like realizing
things about who I was

00:50:23.290 --> 00:50:25.679
and about how I interact.

00:50:25.679 --> 00:50:28.780
Like, the way that I
interacted with my faith

00:50:28.780 --> 00:50:33.630
and my belief system and
what that meant about me

00:50:33.630 --> 00:50:36.940
as a person then, what it means for me now

00:50:39.440 --> 00:50:43.510
and, well, there's one tweet from Holly

00:50:43.510 --> 00:50:44.480
that I wanted to read.

00:50:44.480 --> 00:50:46.587
She said, "I, too, was raised evangelical

00:50:46.587 --> 00:50:49.383
"and I'm an Enneagram one," like me.

00:50:50.417 --> 00:50:52.457
"I struggled my whole life trying to be a,

00:50:52.457 --> 00:50:56.327
"quote, better Christian, and
it wasn't until after college

00:50:56.327 --> 00:50:58.477
"that I realized I was exhausted

00:50:58.477 --> 00:51:01.407
"from living with this internal struggle."

00:51:04.760 --> 00:51:08.920
This process of putting this
out there has really helped me

00:51:08.920 --> 00:51:13.920
begin to understand to a new
level my own internal struggle

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:18.660
and that there is absolutely
a level of exhaustion

00:51:18.660 --> 00:51:22.040
that I did not know that
was there associated with

00:51:24.160 --> 00:51:28.073
my perfectionism, my underlying anxiety.

00:51:30.504 --> 00:51:35.090
And again, I know that
there's a lot of anxiety there

00:51:35.090 --> 00:51:39.000
and I and it's not all from my
experience within the church.

00:51:39.000 --> 00:51:40.503
It's from a lot of things.

00:51:41.890 --> 00:51:46.370
And so, it really provided
the occasion for me

00:51:46.370 --> 00:51:51.370
to say, you know what, I
am going to go to therapy.

00:51:53.214 --> 00:51:55.097
I'm not just gonna talk about it.

00:51:55.097 --> 00:51:56.860
We've talked about it a lot.

00:51:56.860 --> 00:51:58.660
Christy and I have talked about it a lot.

00:51:58.660 --> 00:52:01.790
Some of our closest friends,
we've talked about it for years

00:52:01.790 --> 00:52:06.010
and it almost felt like I was
practically going to therapy.

00:52:06.010 --> 00:52:06.843
But that's also--

00:52:06.843 --> 00:52:08.077
But that's so wrong.

00:52:08.077 --> 00:52:10.250
No, that's so Link.

00:52:10.250 --> 00:52:11.083
Yeah.

00:52:11.083 --> 00:52:13.190
You have this, and I can't come up

00:52:13.190 --> 00:52:14.023
of maybe other examples.
I was like, I'm not opposed

00:52:14.023 --> 00:52:15.040
to it, I just--

00:52:15.040 --> 00:52:17.850
But you have this
tendency that I've noticed

00:52:17.850 --> 00:52:20.280
throughout our lives together that like,

00:52:20.280 --> 00:52:22.490
sometimes, just like when
somebody close to you

00:52:22.490 --> 00:52:25.800
kinda starts doing something
or is interested in something,

00:52:25.800 --> 00:52:30.620
you're just like, yeah, yeah,
I'm, by proxy, interested.

00:52:30.620 --> 00:52:31.730
I am, by proxy.

00:52:31.730 --> 00:52:34.680
So, I think you've been
experiencing the benefits of therapy

00:52:34.680 --> 00:52:37.590
because the people you're
closest with have been going.

00:52:37.590 --> 00:52:38.740
You pick up on a lot.

00:52:38.740 --> 00:52:39.603
Right.

00:52:39.603 --> 00:52:41.930
And it's not like we
don't read and discuss

00:52:41.930 --> 00:52:43.890
a lot of the same books.

00:52:43.890 --> 00:52:46.880
But last night, ironically,

00:52:46.880 --> 00:52:48.240
the same time you were at therapy,

00:52:48.240 --> 00:52:50.780
I was at my first therapy appointment

00:52:50.780 --> 00:52:55.700
and I don't think without
going through this process,

00:52:55.700 --> 00:52:58.860
I would have pulled the trigger

00:52:58.860 --> 00:53:00.763
on setting up that appointment.

00:53:05.570 --> 00:53:06.580
A first therapy,

00:53:06.580 --> 00:53:09.203
I'm gonna sit here and give
you a play-by-play but,

00:53:12.240 --> 00:53:14.520
one hour is not a lot when
you're trying to get it

00:53:14.520 --> 00:53:17.010
and when you have as much as I have

00:53:17.010 --> 00:53:18.510
that you're trying to get out.

00:53:19.840 --> 00:53:24.720
But I'm very excited
to have taken that step

00:53:24.720 --> 00:53:28.680
and to me, it's a tangible
step for me, personally,

00:53:28.680 --> 00:53:30.113
as a result of,

00:53:31.126 --> 00:53:34.630
to follow through on the
commitment that I made to myself

00:53:34.630 --> 00:53:37.700
and just share it at the
end of my story that was,

00:53:37.700 --> 00:53:42.700
I do wanna work at understanding
and loving myself more

00:53:43.300 --> 00:53:48.300
and explaining my capacity
to love others more

00:53:48.910 --> 00:53:53.260
and also to pursue faith.

00:53:53.260 --> 00:53:54.960
To me, those things are connected.

00:53:56.025 --> 00:54:00.490
I don't think if I can't
start to love myself more,

00:54:02.790 --> 00:54:07.307
I don't know if I can
receive any love from God,

00:54:10.130 --> 00:54:14.380
if that love is there to
be received, you know?

00:54:14.380 --> 00:54:15.213
So,

00:54:18.965 --> 00:54:22.630
again, I'm just getting
started but I'm very,

00:54:22.630 --> 00:54:24.000
I'm excited.

00:54:24.000 --> 00:54:29.000
I know there's a lot of
pain associated with therapy

00:54:29.080 --> 00:54:33.250
and I also know that I have a lot of,

00:54:33.250 --> 00:54:36.170
that there's this fear

00:54:38.120 --> 00:54:41.150
that's somewhere in me that
then, on the underside of that,

00:54:41.150 --> 00:54:43.603
there's things that I've
been afraid to explore.

00:54:45.690 --> 00:54:50.480
But my hope and my excitement

00:54:50.480 --> 00:54:55.480
to grow and to work at it

00:54:58.140 --> 00:54:59.560
overrides the fear

00:54:59.560 --> 00:55:03.270
that I think that maybe
has always been there.

00:55:03.270 --> 00:55:07.763
And I think this process
has helped me realize.

00:55:08.870 --> 00:55:09.703
So.

00:55:11.310 --> 00:55:13.160
And again, to hear,

00:55:13.160 --> 00:55:16.100
like Holly's tweet that
like she related to that,

00:55:16.100 --> 00:55:19.530
it was just hearing that somebody related,

00:55:19.530 --> 00:55:21.320
then kinda doubled back on itself

00:55:21.320 --> 00:55:23.970
and helped me understand
myself even better,

00:55:23.970 --> 00:55:26.830
hearing it in somebody else's words.

00:55:26.830 --> 00:55:28.080
It's like, well, this is how I feel

00:55:28.080 --> 00:55:30.220
and this is the words
that I would put it in

00:55:30.220 --> 00:55:31.513
of being exhausted.

00:55:32.810 --> 00:55:34.970
It's like, wow, yeah, that is me.

00:55:34.970 --> 00:55:37.950
So it's already come back

00:55:41.060 --> 00:55:42.333
and I just feel,

00:55:44.129 --> 00:55:46.133
I'm grateful that we've done it.

00:55:47.140 --> 00:55:49.543
Just for me, personally.

00:55:54.400 --> 00:55:58.950
I cannot own how other
people process it for them,

00:55:58.950 --> 00:56:00.930
that's on them.

00:56:00.930 --> 00:56:04.210
Well, if we are gonna answer a question,

00:56:04.210 --> 00:56:07.893
I think you're kinda getting
very close to it already.

00:56:10.270 --> 00:56:13.453
A question that I saw a
number of times was why,

00:56:15.840 --> 00:56:16.943
why tell this story?

00:56:20.740 --> 00:56:22.350
It may be even more specifically,

00:56:22.350 --> 00:56:24.490
there were people who were
like why tell this story?

00:56:24.490 --> 00:56:25.703
What if you're wrong?

00:56:27.130 --> 00:56:29.790
What if you're wrong and you're leading

00:56:31.560 --> 00:56:33.253
an untold number of people,

00:56:34.350 --> 00:56:36.550
depending on the
popularity of this podcast,

00:56:39.100 --> 00:56:40.653
down the wrong path?

00:56:41.630 --> 00:56:42.630
Do you really want that?

00:56:42.630 --> 00:56:44.783
Do you really want that on your shoulders?

00:56:45.630 --> 00:56:47.330
You wanna be responsible for that?

00:56:49.520 --> 00:56:51.160
I think that most of those comments

00:56:51.160 --> 00:56:53.570
are coming from people
who haven't been listening

00:56:53.570 --> 00:56:56.340
to the podcast and I don't
just mean the episodes,

00:56:56.340 --> 00:56:58.550
I mean the podcast as a whole.

00:56:58.550 --> 00:56:59.490
Yeah.

00:56:59.490 --> 00:57:01.320
Because I think that
anyone who's been on board

00:57:01.320 --> 00:57:06.320
for any length of time has
sort of seen this coming

00:57:06.900 --> 00:57:10.250
because we have gotten
increasingly personal

00:57:10.250 --> 00:57:12.700
and so listen, some people are
like, I would never do that,

00:57:12.700 --> 00:57:16.970
I would never talk about what
is happening to me in therapy

00:57:18.040 --> 00:57:20.207
and have thousands of people listen to it.

00:57:20.207 --> 00:57:22.283
Listen, I get that, I respect that.

00:57:25.263 --> 00:57:28.140
And I don't exactly
know why this is the way

00:57:28.140 --> 00:57:31.400
that we've chosen to do what we're doing.

00:57:31.400 --> 00:57:32.933
In some ways,

00:57:35.220 --> 00:57:37.800
this podcast has become therapy,

00:57:37.800 --> 00:57:39.390
it has become very therapeutic, right?

00:57:39.390 --> 00:57:40.540
Because we talk about,

00:57:40.540 --> 00:57:41.970
I talked to my therapist in the way

00:57:41.970 --> 00:57:46.200
that I talked to you guys, on here, right?

00:57:46.200 --> 00:57:47.450
It's just very much like,

00:57:49.255 --> 00:57:52.510
you kinda just start talking
and see what happens.

00:57:53.450 --> 00:57:57.693
And I don't know if there's
just this increased desire

00:57:58.590 --> 00:58:02.110
for vulnerability and being
able to just communicate that

00:58:02.110 --> 00:58:05.070
with a wider audience, I don't
know what the motivations are

00:58:05.070 --> 00:58:06.870
and I don't know exactly how we got here.

00:58:06.870 --> 00:58:11.210
I just know that, like you said,

00:58:11.210 --> 00:58:14.210
the idea of continuing to
kinda pull back the curtain

00:58:14.210 --> 00:58:19.210
and be like this is just
two guys trying to live life

00:58:19.510 --> 00:58:24.190
and figure it out and if you
wanna be on the conversation,

00:58:24.190 --> 00:58:25.900
listen in on the conversation of two guys

00:58:25.900 --> 00:58:27.760
who've known each other
forever, who are best friends,

00:58:27.760 --> 00:58:30.170
who work together, who
are husbands and fathers

00:58:30.170 --> 00:58:32.130
and now, people who've deconstructed

00:58:32.130 --> 00:58:34.033
from their former Christian faith.

00:58:35.640 --> 00:58:37.040
You wanna be along for the ride,

00:58:37.040 --> 00:58:40.540
then let's do it, let's do it together.

00:58:40.540 --> 00:58:43.820
But because it had gotten,

00:58:43.820 --> 00:58:45.570
the tone had gotten so

00:58:47.760 --> 00:58:50.460
vulnerable and there was so
much about self-disclosure

00:58:52.026 --> 00:58:56.230
and because it was so
related to our careers,

00:58:56.230 --> 00:58:58.540
like we can't honestly talk about

00:59:00.380 --> 00:59:03.430
why we do what we do as an actual career

00:59:03.430 --> 00:59:05.090
without talking about how we got here

00:59:05.090 --> 00:59:06.790
and telling the full story

00:59:06.790 --> 00:59:09.430
and we honestly can't tell the full story

00:59:09.430 --> 00:59:13.910
without putting our
perspective on those years

00:59:15.110 --> 00:59:17.220
into perspective, which then requires us

00:59:17.220 --> 00:59:19.852
to tell our story, personally.

00:59:19.852 --> 00:59:23.210
It all kinda fit together.

00:59:23.210 --> 00:59:28.200
But I think another answer
is that we are discovering

00:59:28.200 --> 00:59:32.163
that when you are vulnerable,

00:59:33.320 --> 00:59:38.320
it opens you up to more
love and more empathy,

00:59:40.210 --> 00:59:45.080
for, again, for yourself
and for your best friend

00:59:45.080 --> 00:59:47.410
and for other people.

00:59:47.410 --> 00:59:49.123
What I just described it like,

00:59:51.500 --> 00:59:53.873
I share my story for me,

00:59:54.993 --> 00:59:59.993
it helped other people and
then that response helped me.

01:00:01.580 --> 01:00:06.090
It's just the way,

01:00:06.090 --> 01:00:07.960
people look at it like a show

01:00:07.960 --> 01:00:10.820
and yes, we have to set
boundaries for ourselves

01:00:10.820 --> 01:00:12.540
and we have to be like the thing

01:00:12.540 --> 01:00:13.740
we talked about a few minutes ago,

01:00:13.740 --> 01:00:15.500
about like whether it's on the date

01:00:15.500 --> 01:00:19.050
or like our version of
being the preacher but,

01:00:19.050 --> 01:00:21.540
so yeah, we have to be protective

01:00:21.540 --> 01:00:22.710
and we have to set boundaries

01:00:22.710 --> 01:00:25.740
and we're gonna learn that as we go

01:00:27.190 --> 01:00:31.163
but we're able to,

01:00:32.720 --> 01:00:33.860
and I'm grateful that we're able

01:00:33.860 --> 01:00:37.280
to have an honest conversation
in front of a microphone

01:00:37.280 --> 01:00:39.683
that we would have if it wasn't here.

01:00:41.690 --> 01:00:44.450
We've talked about how
we have them a lot more

01:00:44.450 --> 01:00:47.080
because they're here than if
the microphone is not here

01:00:47.080 --> 01:00:48.123
but then we also,

01:00:50.940 --> 01:00:55.913
what I'm saying is we're
conducting an honest friendship.

01:00:57.178 --> 01:01:00.160
It just happens to be
recorded and I'm just,

01:01:00.160 --> 01:01:03.310
what people do with it is their business

01:01:04.640 --> 01:01:09.640
but I we also experience
a greater capacity

01:01:10.210 --> 01:01:12.283
for connection with,

01:01:14.130 --> 01:01:15.740
and love with an audience

01:01:15.740 --> 01:01:17.950
because it's not just some people sitting

01:01:17.950 --> 01:01:21.700
in chairs listening,
it's individual people

01:01:21.700 --> 01:01:24.040
who were then contributing
to a conversation.

01:01:24.040 --> 01:01:26.150
It's not like we're going on television.

01:01:26.150 --> 01:01:29.450
It's a different thing, you know?

01:01:29.450 --> 01:01:33.090
And the nature of the
conversation has been

01:01:38.480 --> 01:01:39.680
really refreshing.

01:01:39.680 --> 01:01:44.680
And I wanna read this one thing.

01:01:45.860 --> 01:01:49.900
On the Discord channel
of the Mythical Society,

01:01:49.900 --> 01:01:52.530
which is, if you don't
know, that's our fan club

01:01:52.530 --> 01:01:54.540
and yes, you gotta pay for
it, I'm not promoting it

01:01:54.540 --> 01:01:55.940
but I'm just saying it like,

01:01:58.770 --> 01:02:01.260
I was just so encouraged
by this one exchange

01:02:01.260 --> 01:02:02.760
and I'm not gonna read the whole thing

01:02:02.760 --> 01:02:07.760
but there was this
16-year-old guy who was,

01:02:09.090 --> 01:02:13.500
he was processing our "Ear Biscuits"

01:02:13.500 --> 01:02:16.040
and he just shared that

01:02:23.259 --> 01:02:25.700
he's had a devout set of beliefs,

01:02:25.700 --> 01:02:27.833
the specifics of which aren't pertinent,

01:02:30.470 --> 01:02:33.730
and that he was impacted
by our conversation

01:02:33.730 --> 01:02:36.680
and there was parts of it
that kinda shook him up

01:02:36.680 --> 01:02:38.800
and he was just sharing that,

01:02:38.800 --> 01:02:40.963
not with us but just in the Discord.

01:02:41.830 --> 01:02:46.830
And then a response from one
particular mythical beast,

01:02:49.260 --> 01:02:50.763
I just wanna read part of it.

01:02:53.580 --> 01:02:54.537
Well, I'll read the whole thing.

01:02:54.537 --> 01:02:56.127
"I remember being there as a teenager

01:02:56.127 --> 01:02:57.747
"so I definitely know
what you're going through.

01:02:57.747 --> 01:03:01.597
"My best advice is just to trust yourself

01:03:01.597 --> 01:03:03.907
"and to remember that
there's nothing wrong

01:03:03.907 --> 01:03:07.127
"with questioning and
researching on your own

01:03:07.127 --> 01:03:09.397
"and coming to your own conclusions.

01:03:09.397 --> 01:03:12.127
"Remember that no matter
what your religion teaches,

01:03:12.127 --> 01:03:16.327
"there is nothing wrong with
questioning or having doubts

01:03:16.327 --> 01:03:18.297
"and that whatever conclusions you come to

01:03:18.297 --> 01:03:20.287
"or choices that you make,

01:03:20.287 --> 01:03:23.677
"make sure that you're
making them for yourself

01:03:23.677 --> 01:03:24.873
"and no one else.

01:03:26.017 --> 01:03:27.557
"You may have people try to guilt you

01:03:27.557 --> 01:03:30.167
"into making a certain decision
or believe in a certain way

01:03:30.167 --> 01:03:33.137
"because they want you to
believe the way they do.

01:03:33.137 --> 01:03:36.487
"I know I definitely did but ultimately,

01:03:36.487 --> 01:03:40.117
"no one but you can
decide what you believe."

01:03:42.290 --> 01:03:43.387
And then the response was,

01:03:43.387 --> 01:03:45.960
"Yeah, that's what I need to hold on to."

01:03:45.960 --> 01:03:48.050
And then there was some more there.

01:03:48.050 --> 01:03:49.077
And it says, "If you need support,

01:03:49.077 --> 01:03:50.757
"I know there are a ton
of people in this server

01:03:50.757 --> 01:03:52.857
"who would be happy to talk or listen

01:03:52.857 --> 01:03:54.187
"and if there's anything in particular

01:03:54.187 --> 01:03:55.417
"you're struggling with,

01:03:55.417 --> 01:03:57.907
"we might be able to provide resources."

01:04:00.467 --> 01:04:02.970
"Again, I really appreciate
it," was the response.

01:04:02.970 --> 01:04:06.753
And I think there are a
couple of things there.

01:04:09.890 --> 01:04:12.550
It just seems like that's the message

01:04:12.550 --> 01:04:15.973
that I talk to my kids about, you know?

01:04:19.690 --> 01:04:23.298
And it is such a big deal to say okay,

01:04:23.298 --> 01:04:26.410
you have to make these
decisions for yourself.

01:04:26.410 --> 01:04:28.220
No one can make these decisions for you.

01:04:28.220 --> 01:04:32.060
They can but you really need

01:04:32.060 --> 01:04:33.330
to make these decisions for yourself,

01:04:33.330 --> 01:04:36.320
no matter what it is, you're
gonna be loved and supported

01:04:36.320 --> 01:04:38.340
by this community,
which is my second point

01:04:38.340 --> 01:04:43.040
that I've just blown away
that that one example

01:04:43.040 --> 01:04:45.040
is repeated again and again that there's

01:04:47.380 --> 01:04:51.310
so many people in our community

01:04:51.310 --> 01:04:55.100
and then it gives me hope
for humanity at large

01:04:55.100 --> 01:05:00.100
that there's that level of love
and support for people that,

01:05:00.387 --> 01:05:03.090
even if you're not talking
about the specifics

01:05:03.090 --> 01:05:05.490
of what do I agree with you on this

01:05:05.490 --> 01:05:07.740
or are we on the same
page or are you right

01:05:07.740 --> 01:05:09.610
or are you wrong?

01:05:09.610 --> 01:05:14.610
But it's love and
support and encouragement

01:05:15.300 --> 01:05:20.300
to deal with those things in
the most healthy way possible.

01:05:21.310 --> 01:05:23.763
These issues that are so important.

01:05:27.852 --> 01:05:30.160
And I was just grateful for that.

01:05:30.160 --> 01:05:31.460
And that's why we don't,

01:05:34.700 --> 01:05:36.840
we don't want the conversation to devolve

01:05:36.840 --> 01:05:39.720
into an argument, right?

01:05:39.720 --> 01:05:40.553
Yeah.

01:05:40.553 --> 01:05:43.093
About the finer points of theology.

01:05:45.775 --> 01:05:47.490
And again it's not that I don't think,

01:05:47.490 --> 01:05:48.840
I just wanna be just really clear.

01:05:48.840 --> 01:05:51.520
It's not that we don't
think that you having

01:05:51.520 --> 01:05:52.770
your own answers

01:05:54.250 --> 01:05:58.363
as close to the truth as
possible is not important.

01:05:59.820 --> 01:06:02.140
As I hope, I demonstrated in my story,

01:06:02.140 --> 01:06:05.100
it's like because those
answers are important

01:06:05.100 --> 01:06:08.400
is why I don't believe like
I used to believe, right?

01:06:08.400 --> 01:06:10.240
It isn't that those theological points

01:06:10.240 --> 01:06:13.040
and examining those
things are not important,

01:06:13.040 --> 01:06:17.010
they're very important but
the moment that they become

01:06:17.010 --> 01:06:19.680
a source of contention between people

01:06:20.720 --> 01:06:23.990
and the moment that you take
love out of your interaction,

01:06:23.990 --> 01:06:25.040
you're missing the point,

01:06:25.040 --> 01:06:27.630
regardless of what side
of the issue you're on.

01:06:27.630 --> 01:06:32.180
I think that just the idea,

01:06:32.180 --> 01:06:34.370
because basically, what
you're talking about

01:06:34.370 --> 01:06:39.370
is that the most, and
this is a cliche and corny

01:06:39.860 --> 01:06:42.600
but the most important
thing is love, right?

01:06:42.600 --> 01:06:46.490
It's like what is my ethic

01:06:46.490 --> 01:06:49.723
or what are my ethics at this point?

01:06:51.100 --> 01:06:52.550
It's love.

01:06:52.550 --> 01:06:55.850
and I actually think
that asking that question

01:06:55.850 --> 01:07:00.310
in any given situation, what
is the most loving response?

01:07:00.310 --> 01:07:03.490
What is the most loving way
forward in this situation

01:07:03.490 --> 01:07:05.253
is a pretty good guide.

01:07:08.870 --> 01:07:10.980
And incidentally, that's very consistent

01:07:10.980 --> 01:07:13.393
with what Jesus taught, you know?

01:07:14.320 --> 01:07:16.723
Faith, hope and love, the
greatest of these is love.

01:07:19.870 --> 01:07:22.110
I think that regardless of where you're at

01:07:22.110 --> 01:07:24.630
and where you come down
on these particular issues

01:07:24.630 --> 01:07:26.927
and what you think
about the nature of life

01:07:26.927 --> 01:07:28.453
and the nature of eternity,

01:07:30.120 --> 01:07:34.663
you're not gonna go wrong if
you kinda fall back on that.

01:07:38.266 --> 01:07:41.236
What is the most loving
thing that I could do

01:07:41.236 --> 01:07:42.069
in this situation?

01:07:42.069 --> 01:07:45.230
And like Link said, I think
that it's been super encouraging

01:07:45.230 --> 01:07:47.820
that the vast majority of the conversation

01:07:47.820 --> 01:07:50.250
has been loving and respectful.

01:07:50.250 --> 01:07:55.090
We have an incredible
community, we value that a lot

01:07:55.090 --> 01:07:59.180
and that's the conversation
that loving conversation

01:07:59.180 --> 01:08:02.320
is what we want to continue to engender.

01:08:02.320 --> 01:08:05.280
And it's not about two guys who are,

01:08:05.280 --> 01:08:08.518
listen, we may have engineering
degrees but we're just,

01:08:08.518 --> 01:08:11.380
we're YouTube comedians.

01:08:11.380 --> 01:08:12.213
That's what we are.

01:08:12.213 --> 01:08:14.670
We're not authorities
on any of these issues.

01:08:14.670 --> 01:08:16.930
If you're coming to either one of us

01:08:16.930 --> 01:08:19.950
to figure out what you
think about your faith,

01:08:19.950 --> 01:08:21.773
there are much better sources.

01:08:24.304 --> 01:08:27.180
And so, that was that was
not the intention at all

01:08:27.180 --> 01:08:29.460
in telling our stories
was you to be convinced,

01:08:29.460 --> 01:08:30.453
that we were right.

01:08:31.670 --> 01:08:34.057
It's just very much hey,
this is what our process was,

01:08:34.057 --> 01:08:36.820
this is what our story was.

01:08:36.820 --> 01:08:39.193
You should be doing
your own investigation.

01:08:42.142 --> 01:08:44.809
But we just wanna be about love.

01:08:47.660 --> 01:08:48.910
It's that simple.

01:08:48.910 --> 01:08:51.570
And on that note, can I
recommend a children's book?

01:08:51.570 --> 01:08:52.820
Sure.

01:08:52.820 --> 01:08:54.300
I was reading this book to Lando.

01:08:54.300 --> 01:08:55.610
He pulled it off his shelf

01:08:55.610 --> 01:08:57.580
and this is my Recs in Effect this week.

01:08:57.580 --> 01:08:59.850
It's called the "The Three Questions"

01:08:59.850 --> 01:09:04.850
written and illustrated by
Jon, J-O-N, J. Muth, M-U-T-H.

01:09:08.347 --> 01:09:11.640
"The Three Questions" is based
on a story by Leo Tolstoy,

01:09:11.640 --> 01:09:15.330
it's a short story that I had never read.

01:09:15.330 --> 01:09:19.460
And so as I was reading this
children's book to Lando,

01:09:19.460 --> 01:09:21.533
I was just moved.

01:09:23.150 --> 01:09:27.290
And it's very pertinent
to the conversations

01:09:27.290 --> 01:09:29.070
that we've been having
and it was just like,

01:09:29.070 --> 01:09:32.670
it felt like a faith moment,
I'll be honest that I was,

01:09:32.670 --> 01:09:34.420
I mean like Lando started
looking at me weird

01:09:34.420 --> 01:09:36.657
because I was like
tearing up and I was like,

01:09:36.657 --> 01:09:38.765
"This book, this is amazing."

01:09:38.765 --> 01:09:42.350
There's some wisdom in this

01:09:42.350 --> 01:09:44.650
and I didn't realize that it was based

01:09:44.650 --> 01:09:48.200
on a Leo Tolstoy a short story
until we were done reading it

01:09:48.200 --> 01:09:50.730
but like I could sense

01:09:50.730 --> 01:09:53.293
that there was some depth to this thing.

01:09:56.020 --> 01:09:58.280
So, you know what, I'm not
gonna tell you about it

01:09:58.280 --> 01:10:00.313
but I recommend, if you,

01:10:00.313 --> 01:10:03.090
Lando's nine, he was very much into it

01:10:04.580 --> 01:10:06.400
but you don't have to
have kids to read it.

01:10:06.400 --> 01:10:11.080
I actually recommend this
children's book adaptation

01:10:12.070 --> 01:10:14.996
instead of just listening to on Audible

01:10:14.996 --> 01:10:19.420
or reading the Leo Tolstoy
short story of the same name,

01:10:19.420 --> 01:10:21.164
though I do recommend that story as well

01:10:21.164 --> 01:10:23.690
but the way that it's
turned, is more modernized

01:10:23.690 --> 01:10:27.043
and it's a little bit more existential is,

01:10:28.370 --> 01:10:30.100
it's very powerful.

01:10:30.100 --> 01:10:33.040
So, that's my recommendation.

01:10:33.040 --> 01:10:34.760
Will you read it to me?
A kid's book.

01:10:34.760 --> 01:10:36.060
Will you come over to my
house to read it to me

01:10:36.060 --> 01:10:36.900
and tuck me in tonight?

01:10:36.900 --> 01:10:38.340
Gladly, yeah.

01:10:38.340 --> 01:10:39.233
Good.
Yeah.

